Oculomotor Integrator, Degeneracy, and Sloppy Models velocity→position → bilateral integrator → dominant \(\lambda =1\)→ weight-family degeneracy → nonlinear synapse fit → least squares/Hessian Explain why integrating an eye-velocity command requires a persistent neural state after a saccade, and connect bilateral excitation/inhibition to left–right position tuning. Derive the characteristic equation, eigenvalue-one constraint, and compensating weight family for a general \(2	imes 2\) recurrent matrix, while also checking transverse stability. Use the inverse of a measured F–I curve and fixed synaptic nonlinearities to construct a design matrix, turning an apparently nonlinear circuit fit into row-wise least squares. Use the eigendecomposition of a quadratic loss Hessian to explain stiff and sloppy directions, parameter compensation, and an ensemble of adequate models. Distinguish low dimensionality in neural-state space, sloppiness in parameter space, measurement uncertainty, and genuine biological degeneracy. The oculomotor integrator must retain eye position after a saccade, so the network needs a long-time-scale mode near \(\lambda =1\). For a general \(2	imes 2\) W, perfect integration imposes only \(\det(I-W)=0\), leaving a many-to-one family of weights; parameter degeneracy is still stronger in complex biological models. Once measured tuning and synaptic nonlinearities are fixed, fitting can be rewritten as linear regression. Hessian eigenvalues separate a few stiff combinations from many sloppy ones, motivating an adequate-model ensemble rather than a unique best fit. Mistaking persistent eye position for a sensory afterimage rather than an integral of velocity. Requiring \(\lambda =1\) but failing to check stability of the other modes. Using the division-based family formula even when \(W_{12}=0\). Treating a many-to-one parameter mapping as measurement noise. Confusing low dimensionality in neural-state space with sloppiness in parameter space. Interpreting a sloppy parameter as biologically irrelevant. Reporting only best-fit weights without uncertainty or an ensemble. Low dimensionality in neural-state space and sloppiness in parameter space must be distinguished; they are different ideas in different spaces. eye-position integration Z t \(x(t) = x(0) + v(t' )dt'\) 0 velocity command eigenvalue 1 condition \(\det(I - W ) = 0\) perfect linear integrator weight-compensation family \(W_{21} = (1 - W_{11} )(1 - W_{22} )/W_{12}\) \(W_{12}
