e 0\). Special structures must be analyzed directly with \(\det(I-W)=0\). No, division by zero is invalid. The integrator condition becomes \((1-W_{11})(1-W_{22})=0\), so at least one of W11 and W22 must equal 1. What does a large Hessian eigenvalue mean? It does not mean that one corresponding synapse is large, nor that the direction is a principal component of neural activity. Loss has high curvature along the corresponding parameter combination, so a small parameter change substantially worsens the fit; that direction is stiff. If position returns to center after the velocity command with a time constant of 5 s, is this still an integrator? It is a leaky rather than perfect integrator. Behavior may appear persistent for periods shorter than 5 s, but a long delay reveals measurable drift. This is the distinction tested in Eye-velocity integration, persistent position, and the bilateral circuit. Worked example — Accumulate two saccade commands: Start at \(x=0 \mathrm{deg}\). Move right at 30 deg/s for 0.08 s, then left at -20 deg/s for 0.05 s. Ignoring leak, find the final position. Which result or conclusion follows from these stated inputs? Worked setup: Start at \(x=0 \mathrm{deg}\). Move right at 30 deg/s for 0.08 s, then left at -20 deg/s for 0.05 s. Ignoring leak, find the final position. The first change is \(30	imes 0.08=+2.4 \mathrm{deg}\). The second change is \(-20	imes 0.05=-1.0 \mathrm{deg}\). The final position is \(x=0+2.4-1.0=+1.4 \mathrm{deg}\), still rightward. Signs of velocity must match left–right direction. The total command area \(2.4-1.0=1.4 \mathrm{deg}\) provides a direct check. Which statement gives the core conclusion developed in “Eye-velocity integration, persistent position, and the bilateral circuit”? The time integral of velocity gives position change. The module's worked example and checks support the same conclusion. Which statement gives the important constraint or comparison developed in “Eye-velocity integration, persistent position, and the bilateral circuit”? Recurrent positive feedback sustains a persistent state after the command ends. The module's worked example and checks support the same conclusion. When applying “Eye-velocity integration, persistent position, and the bilateral circuit” to a new experiment or dataset, which principle must still hold? The time integral of velocity gives position change. The variables may change in a new setting, but the model must still satisfy this constraint. Which units check correctly constrains the derivation “From velocity samples to a continuous position integral”? v×dt gives deg, so the integral has the same units as x. In a rate-coordinate neural equation, k must supply any missing units. As \(	au _{\mathrm{eff}}	o \infty\) with appropriately scaled input, the system approaches a perfect integrator; with finite \(	au _{\mathrm{eff}}\), \(\alpha\) drifts exponentially under zero input. These checks test both dimensional consistency and limiting behavior. What is the best response to the guiding question: How does a brief saccade command become an eye-position code that persists after the command disappears? Moving the eye requires phasic motor drive to generate velocity and tonic drive to oppose orbital restoring forces and hold the new position. The course abstracts the distributed brainstem and cerebellar network as a neural integrator: its input is the eye-velocity command \(v(t)\), and its output state approximates \(x(t)=x(0)+\int v(t')dt'\). Ideally, x retains its latest value when v returns to zero; if the network leaks, position slowly drifts back toward center. The source diagram contains excitatory populations EL and ER and inhibitory populations IL and IR in the left and right hemispheres. Their position-tuning curves have opposite directions: at rightward positions, firing rises in one population and falls in the other. Mutual excitation can prolong the memory mode, while crossed inhibition organizes the left–right push–pull pattern into a signed coordinate. This is a functional architecture; arrows alone do not reveal the strength of each real synapse. What happens if the same constraint gives \(\lambda _{2}=1.1\)? The memory mode still has \(\lambda =1\), but the transverse mode's full growth rate is \((1.1-1)/	au >0\). Any small error grows, so the full network is not a stable, usable integrator. This is the distinction tested in General \(2	imes 2\) weights, eigenvalue one, and a compensating family. Worked example — Solve for the fourth weight and check stability: Let \(W_{11}=0.8\), \(W_{22}=0.7\), and \(W_{12}=0.2\). Find W21 so that one mode is a perfect integrator, then check the other mode. Which result or conclusion follows from these stated inputs? Worked setup: Let \(W_{11}=0.8\), \(W_{22}=0.7\), and \(W_{12}=0.2\). Find W21 so that one mode is a perfect integrator, then check the other mode. \(W_{21}=(0.2	imes 0.3)/0.2=0.3\). The other eigenvalue is \(0.8+0.7-1=0.5\). Its full transverse coefficient is \(-(1-0.5)/	au =-0.5/	au\), so that mode decays stably. The \(\operatorname{trace} 1.5\) equals 1+0.5, and the determinant 0.5 equals \(1	imes 0.5\), providing two checks. Which statement gives the core conclusion developed in “General \(2	imes 2\) weights, eigenvalue one, and a compensating family”? A perfect linear integrator satisfies \(\det(I-W)=0\). The module's worked example and checks support the same conclusion. Which statement gives the important constraint or comparison developed in “General \(2	imes 2\) weights, eigenvalue one, and a compensating family”? When \(W_{12}
