e 0\), \(W_{21}=(1-W_{11})(1-W_{22})/W_{12}\). The variables may change in a new setting, but the model must still satisfy this constraint. Which units check correctly constrains the derivation “From the characteristic polynomial to an integrator family”? \(\det(I-W)\) is dimensionless. \(	au\) appears only when converting an eigenvalue gap into a time constant. As \(W_{12}	o 0\), the family formula becomes numerically unbounded, signaling that one should return to the undivided constraint rather than infer an infinite synapse. These checks test both dimensional consistency and limiting behavior. Why does fitting become linear after fixing the nonlinearities even though the neuron response itself remains nonlinear? \(S_{j}\) and \(F_{i}^{-1}\) already apply nonlinear transformations to the data. Only the unknown weights linearly combine those transformed features; the full input–output mapping remains nonlinear. This is the distinction tested in Biological variability and reparameterizing a nonlinear circuit fit. Worked example — Build one design row and predict current: At two positions, an output has features \(x_{1}=[1,2]\) and \(x_{2}=[3,1]\), with an additional bias 1 at both. The weights are \([w_{1},w_{2},b]=[0.5,-1,2]\). Find both predictions. Which result or conclusion follows from these stated inputs? Worked setup: At two positions, an output has features \(x_{1}=[1,2]\) and \(x_{2}=[3,1]\), with an additional bias 1 at both. The weights are \([w_{1},w_{2},b]=[0.5,-1,2]\). Find both predictions. At position 1, the feature vector is (1,2,1) and the prediction is \(0.5	imes 1-1	imes 2+2=0.5\). At position 2, the feature vector is (3,1,1) and the prediction is \(0.5	imes 3-1	imes 1+2=2.5\). With the two samples as columns, \(X=[[1,3],[2,1],[1,1]]\), \(W=[0.5,-1,2]\), and \(WX=[0.5,2.5]\). W has shape \(1	imes 3\) and X has shape \(3	imes 2\), so the output must be \(1	imes 2\). An answer of \(2	imes 1\) indicates that sample orientation was reversed. Which statement gives the core conclusion developed in “Biological variability and reparameterizing a nonlinear circuit fit”? First use \(F_{i}^{-1}\) to convert each target rate into required current. The module's worked example and checks support the same conclusion. Which statement gives the important constraint or comparison developed in “Biological variability and reparameterizing a nonlinear circuit fit”? Once synaptic nonlinearities are fixed, their outputs become design-matrix features. The module's worked example and checks support the same conclusion. When applying “Biological variability and reparameterizing a nonlinear circuit fit” to a new experiment or dataset, which principle must still hold? With samples as columns, \(Y=N	imes M\), \(X=D	imes M\), and \(W=N	imes D\). The variables may change in a new setting, but the model must still satisfy this constraint. Which units check correctly constrains the derivation “From a steady-state circuit equation to \(Y=WX\)”? \((N	imes D)(D	imes M)=N	imes M\), matching Y; every column corresponds to the same eye position. If \(F_{i}\) is not invertible or the rate lies on a saturation plateau, \(F_{i}^{-1}\) is not unique and target-current construction fails. These checks test both dimensional consistency and limiting behavior. How does adding a ridge penalty \(\lambda ||w||^{2}/2\) change the Hessian? H becomes \(XX^\mathrm{T}+\lambda I\). Every eigenvalue increases by \(\lambda\), stabilizing the inverse, but the estimate is biased toward zero; regularization must not be mistaken for additional data. This is the distinction tested in Least squares, the closed-form solution, and the Hessian. Worked example — Identifiability from a two-feature Hessian: Let \(X=[[1,0,1],[0,1,1]]\). Compute \(H=XX^\mathrm{T}\) and determine whether it is invertible. Which result or conclusion follows from these stated inputs? Worked setup: Let \(X=[[1,0,1],[0,1,1]]\). Compute \(H=XX^\mathrm{T}\) and determine whether it is invertible. \(H_{11}=1^{2}+0^{2}+1^{2}=2\), and \(H_{22}=0^{2}+1^{2}+1^{2}=2\). \(H_{12}=H_{21}=1	imes 0+0	imes 1+1	imes 1=1\), so \(H=[[2,1],[1,2]]\). \(\det(H)=4-1=3>0\), so H is invertible; its eigenvalues are 3 and 1. The \(\operatorname{trace} 4\) must equal the eigenvalue \(\operatorname{sum} 3+1\), and the determinant 3 must equal the product \(3	imes 1\). Which statement gives the core conclusion developed in “Least squares, the closed-form solution, and the Hessian”? The OLS loss is \(1/2||Y-WX||^{2}\). The module's worked example and checks support the same conclusion. Which statement gives the important constraint or comparison developed in “Least squares, the closed-form solution, and the Hessian”? When the inverse exists, \(\hat{W}=YX^\mathrm{T}(XX^\mathrm{T})^{-1}\). The module's worked example and checks support the same conclusion. When applying “Least squares, the closed-form solution, and the Hessian” to a new experiment or dataset, which principle must still hold? Collinear features make H singular or nearly singular, requiring regularization or new experiments. The variables may change in a new setting, but the model must still satisfy this constraint. Which units check correctly constrains the derivation “From quadratic loss to the OLS closed form”? \((1	imes D)(D	imes M)=1	imes M\), and \(XX^\mathrm{T}\) is D×D. Every term in the loss has squared target-current units. If \(M<D\) or the features are linearly dependent, \(\operatorname{rank}(XX^\mathrm{T})<D\) and the ordinary inverse does not exist. A pseudoinverse selects a minimum-norm solution but does not remove the identifiability problem. These checks test both dimensional consistency and limiting behavior. Why can sloppy not be translated as 'this parameter is unimportant'? A Hessian direction usually combines parameters, and small curvature means only that the current data and loss are insensitive to it. The combination may still be crucial for an unmeasured behavior, a perturbation response, or biological viability. This is the distinction tested in The Hessian eigenbasis, stiff/sloppy directions, and model ensembles. Worked example — Compare two curvature directions: The Hessian eigenvalues are 100 and 0.01, and the allowed \(\Delta L\) is 0.5. Find the maximum displacement along each axis and their ratio. Which result or conclusion follows from these stated inputs? Worked setup: The Hessian eigenvalues are 100 and 0.01, and the allowed \(\Delta L\) is 0.5. Find the maximum displacement along each axis and their ratio. \(2c=1\). The stiff radius is \(\sqrt{1/100}=0.1\). The sloppy radius is \(\sqrt{1/0.01}=10\). The ratio is \(10/0.1=100\); at the same loss tolerance, the sloppy direction permits a displacement 100 times larger. The eigenvalue ratio is \(100/0.01=10000\), so the axis ratio is its square root, 100, not 10000. Which statement gives the core conclusion developed in “The Hessian eigenbasis, stiff/sloppy directions, and model ensembles”? \(\Delta L=1/2\sum \lambda _{k} z_{k}^{2}\) decomposes curvature along parameter eigen-directions. The module's worked example and checks support the same conclusion. Which statement gives the important constraint or comparison developed in “The Hessian eigenbasis, stiff/sloppy directions, and model ensembles”? Large \(\lambda\) is stiff; small \(\lambda\) is sloppy. The module's worked example and checks support the same conclusion. When applying “The Hessian eigenbasis, stiff/sloppy directions, and model ensembles” to a new experiment or dataset, which principle must still hold? \(\Delta L=1/2\sum \lambda _{k} z_{k}^{2}\) decomposes curvature along parameter eigen-directions. The variables may change in a new setting, but the model must still satisfy this constraint. Which units check correctly constrains the derivation “Derive ellipse-axis lengths from curvature”? \(\lambda _{k}\) has units of loss/\(parameter^{2}\), so taking the square root of \(2c/\lambda _{k}\) restores parameter units. If \(\lambda _{k}=0\), the quadratic approximation gives an infinitely flat direction, meaning that the data do not constrain that combination at all. Actual bounds or higher-order terms may truncate it. These checks test both dimensional consistency and limiting behavior. Which interpretation is supported by the schematic “Three fates of an eigenvalue-one integrator”? After input is removed, the fate of the same initial mode amplitude depends only on \(1-\lambda\): when \(\lambda <1\) it leaks; when \(\lambda =1\) the restoring coefficient in the derivative vanishes and memory persists; when \(\lambda >1 a\) small deviation grows. Thus \(\det(I-W)=0\) guarantees only an integrating mode, and the stability of all remaining modes must still be checked. Check the labeled axes, nodes, or event order before mapping the schematic back to the model claim. What does “oculomotor integrator” mean in this lecture? oculomotor integrator: A circuit that converts an eye-velocity command into a persistent eye-position signal. This is the meaning used in this lecture, not every possible use of the term. What does “line attractor” mean in this lecture? line attractor: A continuous family of nearly stable states capable of storing a graded value. This is the meaning used in this lecture, not every possible use of the term. What does “parameter degeneracy” mean in this lecture? parameter degeneracy: The ability of different parameter combinations to produce similar output or function. This is the meaning used in this lecture, not every possible use of the term. What does “identifiability” mean in this lecture? identifiability: The extent to which data uniquely or sufficiently constrain parameters. This is the meaning used in this lecture, not every possible use of the term. What does “Hessian” mean in this lecture? Hessian: The local curvature matrix of loss with respect to parameters. This is the meaning used in this lecture, not every possible use of the term. What does “stiff direction” mean in this lec